File Index

/chat-display-text.ts
Purpose: Cleans only the text that is shown, never the stored raw line: the raw BEL must survive for features/audio/audio-beep.ts and the classifiers. OSC sequences go before BEL because BEL also terminates them. Tests: chat-display-text.test.ts and chat-store.test.ts beside it.

/chat-probe.ts
Purpose: Identifies and parses chat message quotes in text buffers for efficient chat detection and extraction.

/chat-settings-store.ts
Purpose: `unreadDots` is stored only when on (toStored) so an untouched default follows future defaults; the other fields are still written in full. normalizeSettings copies enabledPanes instead of sharing the defaults object, because ChatPane memoises on its identity (shared, a gear toggle left the tabs unchanged). Tests load a fresh module per case since the store caches. Tests: chat-settings-store.test.ts.

/chat-unread-store.ts
Purpose: Module-level on purpose, like chat-store.ts: a ChatPane that unmounts and mounts again must keep the baseline and seen ids or it lights up history it already showed. The baseline is taken once while armed. Rules and tests: chat-unread.ts, chat-unread-store.test.ts.

/chat-unread.ts
Purpose: summarizeMessages replaces ChatPane's counting loop, it does not add a second pass (the list is unbounded and recomputed per message). Own lines count for pane visibility but never as newest, so what you say cannot light a dot.

/dsl-to-ansi.ts
Purpose: Maps DSL color codes to ANSI escape sequences for terminal text formatting.

/strict-chat-classifier.ts
Purpose: Classifies raw chat input into structured subtypes for consistent handling. Speaking a language puts a "(Language)" tag between the verb and the quote ("You clan (Elvish) 'hi'"), but only on in-character channels (say, clan, clan gossip, gossip, kingdom, yell, shout); OOC channels (answer, pray, radio, quest, grats, newbie, OOC, OOC clan/kingdom) never carry one, so their own-echo checks stay plain startsWith. Kingdom puts the tag AFTER the colon ("Kingdom: (Elvish) '…'"), which the " Kingdom: " substring check already handles. An unclassified line is DROPPED in strict mode (runtimeSingleton.ts), so a missed shape loses the line rather than mis-filing it. SHOUT must end its envelope at the opening quote: the taunt skill's "<X> shouts a harsh string of insults at you!" has no quote and is not a shout. Other consumers of the subtype: text-to-speech (communication category, and Arena mode's chat drop) and the highlighter (gossip/cgossip only), so adding a subtype changes what those treat as chat. Tests: strict-chat-classifier.test.ts.
